WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.622
In a lakehouse workflow, we often save curated results as a table.

00:00:04.622 --> 00:00:09.142
This example creates a monthly sales table from raw sales data.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:14.662
The important design choice is the output grain: one row per month and product.

00:00:14.662 --> 00:00:21.657
In a real Spark environment, you would also think about table format, location, and refresh process.

00:00:21.657 --> 00:00:26.887
A lakehouse combines scalable file-based storage with table-management features.

00:00:26.887 --> 00:00:35.612
Delta-style tables add transaction logs, schema controls, and reliable table operations on top of object

00:00:35.612 --> 00:00:36.012
storage.

00:00:36.012 --> 00:00:41.742
Keep raw ingestion, cleaned data, and curated analytical tables conceptually separate so lineage remains

00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:43.397
clear.
